# The player, transcribed verbatim out of src/entities/PlayerSprite.ts.
#
# Nothing about the art changed in the move — same grid, same palette, same
# frames, same modes, same rates. What changed is who owns it: the poses were
# module constants in a .ts file, so redrawing the character meant editing
# TypeScript, and `AnimState` was a hand-written union that could drift from the
# sequence table sitting twenty lines below it. Both are generated from this file
# now, so the twelve states the movement code can produce and the twelve
# sequences that exist are the same list by construction.
#
# ---- ONE ART PIXEL IS ONE WORLD CELL ---------------------------------------
# 2x3 cells = 10x15px at CELL_SIZE 5, which is exactly the collision box: no
# overhang, every pixel a pose uses is inside the hitbox. The art used to be a
# 16x16 grid squeezed into that box — a sprite pixel was 1.25 x 1.5 world px,
# four times finer than the sim cell and not even square, so the character read
# as a smooth hi-res figure pasted onto a chunky cellular world. The grid is the
# cell grid now, and this file cannot express anything else.
#
# ---- THE CHARACTER, AT SIX PIXELS ------------------------------------------
# There is no silhouette to design at 2x3 and no room for a profile, so the
# character is carried by three things and nothing else:
#
#   row 0   HEAD — a single ember pixel. Empty on one side, which is what makes
#           the figure read as facing somewhere rather than as a block.
#   row 1   BODY — two pixels, lit (2) on the leading side and shade (3) on the
#           trailing side. Swapping those two tones flips the read of which way
#           the body's weight is going, which is the only "lean" available.
#   row 2   FEET — ink. Both lit = planted, one lit = mid-stride, both empty =
#           airborne.
#
# Authored facing RIGHT; left is a horizontal flip at draw time. Transparency
# does most of the acting: dropping row 2 lifts the figure off the ground,
# dropping column 0 flattens it against a wall, dropping row 0 and pushing
# everything down squashes it on impact.
#
# ---- SHARED POSES ARE REPEATED, NOT NAMED ----------------------------------
# Several states legitimately land on the same drawing — run's passing pose is
# the idle exhale, jump's apex is the doubleJump tuck, land's and punch's
# recovery are both the neutral stand. Those frames are written out again here
# rather than hoisted into a named intermediate: the runtime's bake cache is
# keyed on the frame text, so identical frames still share ONE baked canvas, and
# the file stays readable as a filmstrip instead of as a symbol table.

@sprite player
name      Player

cellsW    2
cellsH    3

# Six pixels leave no room for shading, so every colour has to do a job on its
# own: two body tones that encode facing, one hot head pixel, one alarm colour,
# one spark, one ink for the feet. Nothing here is decorative. Teal body + gold
# head is the identity — the loudest thing against a world of sand, dirt and
# stone, and an ember survives being one pixel where a face never could.
#
#   0 .        transparent          4 #ffcf5c  ember head — the bloom pass takes it
#   1 #1b1220  ink, planted feet    5 #ff6b57  ember blown red — hurt only
#   2 #3ec8b4  body lit (leading)   6 #fff3c4  spark — dash, punch, swim bubble
#   3 #21877e  body shade (trailing)
pal       . #1b1220 #3ec8b4 #21877e #ffcf5c #ff6b57 #fff3c4

variants  1

# Every sequence below sets its own rate, so the sprite-wide `fps` is never
# consulted; it is left at the schema default rather than authored to a lie.

# --- idle -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ambient, not a loop: a slow breath with an occasional blink on top. The first
# two frames alternate at `fps` (exhale/inhale), and the LAST frame is the
# interrupt — held for blinkFor every blinkEvery. The ember dropping to body
# tone IS the blink; there is no eye to close.
#
# fps is written as the reciprocal of the authored beat, 1/0.62, and the exact
# double for it rather than a rounded 1.613. The runtime computes
# `beat = 1/fps` and then `floor(t / beat)`, and 1/(1/0.62) === 0.62 exactly in
# IEEE754, so the reciprocal round-trips bit-for-bit to the original
# IDLE_BREATH constant. 1.613 does not: it disagrees with the old
# PlayerSprite.idleFrame() on 91 of 600,000 samples over 60s.
seq  state=idle mode=ambient fps=1.6129032258064517 blinkEvery=4.6 blinkFor=0.1 |
  .4
  22
  11

  .4
  32
  11

  .3
  32
  11

# --- run --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Indexed by the run phase the Player advances from real horizontal speed, so
# contacts land on phase 0.0 and 0.5 — which is where the footstep particles are
# emitted. Cadence is the whole cycle: the planted foot alternates between the
# trailing and leading pixel, with both down on the passing beat.
#
# THE TWO PASSING BEATS ARE NOT THE SAME DRAWING ANY MORE, and that is the only
# change to this sequence. They used to be byte-identical, which meant the cycle
# really only had three distinct frames and read as A-B-C-B — a shuffle. Frame 1
# now lifts the body onto BOTH lit pixels: at this size a tone change is the
# only "rise" available, and `22` already means an extended, bright body
# everywhere else in this file (it is the jump launch). Frame 3 keeps the
# shaded `32`, so the near-side pass rides high and the far-side pass rides low
# — which is what a run seen from one side actually does. The tones are NOT
# swapped to `23` on either beat: that reversal is reserved for `skid`, where it
# means the weight is going the other way, and reusing it here would make every
# other stride read as a turnaround.
seq  state=run mode=phase |
  .4
  32
  1.

  .4
  22
  11

  .4
  32
  .1

  .4
  32
  11

# --- skid -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The body tones are SWAPPED, so the lit side trails the facing: the figure
# faces the new direction while its weight still goes the old way, which is
# exactly what a turnaround is. At this size a tone swap is a legible event.
#
# Now a two-frame loop rather than a single held pose: a spark (6) flickers
# under the LEADING foot, which is the one actually scraping when the weight is
# still travelling the other way. Nothing else about the drawing changes between
# the frames, so the state still reads as one fixed posture — it just stops
# being the only sustained state in the set that is completely static, which at
# ten frames a second is the difference between braking and being paused.
seq  state=skid mode=loop fps=10 |
  .4
  23
  11

  .4
  23
  16

# --- jump -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Launch (body extended and bright, trailing foot already gone) into the tuck.
seq  state=jump mode=once fps=9 |
  .4
  22
  .1

  .4
  32
  ..

# --- doubleJump -------------------------------------------------------------
# The one move with a real animation left: the ember travels top -> leading side
# -> bottom across three beats, which reads as a forward roll. Nothing else in
# the set moves the head pixel, so it cannot be confused with the first jump.
# Recovers onto a run contact.
seq  state=doubleJump mode=once fps=12 |
  .4
  32
  ..

  34
  3.
  ..

  33
  .4
  ..

  .4
  32
  .1

# --- fall -------------------------------------------------------------------
# A dim, loose body (both tones shaded) with a foot swinging underneath. Three
# frames on a slow loop so it flails rather than vibrates — the middle frame
# drops BOTH feet, so the leg swings through empty air between the two contacts
# instead of teleporting from one side to the other. Two frames made the fall
# read as a metronome; the odd frame count is what makes it read as loose.
seq  state=fall mode=loop fps=6 |
  .4
  33
  1.

  .4
  33
  ..

  .4
  33
  .1

# --- land -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Impact: the figure loses its top row entirely — the ember drops into the
# middle and the body squashes onto the feet. A third of the height gone in one
# frame is the most violent thing available here.
#
# Three beats now, not two: squash, RISE, neutral. The rise recovers the full
# height but keeps the body on the dim `33` it had while falling, so the figure
# comes back up before it comes back to strength. Cutting straight from the
# squash to the neutral stand made the recovery instantaneous, which threw away
# most of what the squash frame had just paid for.
seq  state=land mode=once fps=14 |
  ..
  .4
  32

  .4
  33
  11

  .4
  32
  11

# --- dash -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sparks trail from the BACK column (behind the facing) and shift between the
# frames, so at 18fps the dash reads as a streak rather than a pose. Three
# frames rather than two: an even count at this rate gives a 9Hz alternation
# that the eye locks onto as a flicker, where an odd one keeps re-landing on a
# different beat and stays a streak. The third frame is the only one with a
# spark on two rows at once — the trail briefly doubling is what sells speed.
seq  state=dash mode=loop fps=18 |
  64
  32
  ..

  .4
  62
  6.

  64
  62
  ..

# --- wallSlide --------------------------------------------------------------
# Column 0 is dropped entirely: the figure squeezes into a one-pixel-wide column
# pressed against the wall it faces. A 1x3 silhouette is unlike anything else in
# the set, so this state is never ambiguous.
seq  state=wallSlide mode=loop fps=6 |
  .4
  .2
  .1

  .4
  .2
  ..

# --- swim -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ember moves into the MIDDLE row with the body behind it — a prone,
# horizontal figure — and a bubble drifts across the top row. Feet never plant.
# Three beats (reach, gather, kick) played [A, B, C, B], so the gather frame
# appears twice and the stroke has a returning half.
seq  state=swim mode=loop fps=6 |
  6.
  34
  3.

  ..
  34
  .3

  .6
  34
  33

  ..
  34
  .3

# --- punch ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wind-up (the body dims and coils, feet set), contact (a spark punched out of
# the leading body pixel), recovery to neutral.
seq  state=punch mode=once fps=14 |
  .4
  33
  11

  .4
  36
  11

  .4
  32
  11

# --- hurt -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ember blows red AND snaps to the trailing column, the body tones reverse,
# and a foot leaves the floor. Three changes at once — the most distinct frame
# in the set, which is right for the state you most need to read instantly.
seq  state=hurt mode=hold |
  5.
  23
  1.
